{"kind":"artifact","text":"internal/app carries the resident ownership claim, at head 0ac0bcf0626436053482aab57d6bbd5651cdc4b2 on request/multi-resident-coordination, rebased onto main f22fa1dd after the #6356 and #6350 merges landed. This replaces the retired candidate 2b6e283b2a7c1979a9b30c0ff7dcb2611eb89ae6, which described the same work at the old base 84de35f6. internal/app/resident.go and internal/app/resident_test.go are byte-identical across the rebase — main changed nothing in this package — so what follows is unchanged except for the head it names.\n\nResidentRef(genesis) names refs/gitseq/resident/\u003cgenesis\u003e. ClaimResident acquires, contests, takes over and refuses; ResidentOwnership.Release withdraws. Acquisition, takeover and release are one primitive, a Git ref update carrying the expected old value, so exactly one process holds the claim at a time. Each claim blob carries a fresh sixteen-byte random nonce, minted on every acquisition attempt including retries, which makes the object id unique to one acquisition and closes A-B-A. Reads are bounded at 8 KiB and contention at eight attempts; an unreadable, malformed, incomplete, or foreign claim is a refusal naming the ref and the recovery command, never a vacancy. Only Dead authorizes a takeover, and Liveness's zero value is Ambiguous, so a prober that decides nothing refuses. PublishResident and ResidentURL keep their signatures and meaning and are documented as endpoint metadata rather than authority.\n\nTests at this head, in internal/app/resident_test.go, each pinning one interleaving through an injected prober so the ordering is exact rather than timed: twelve simultaneous starters yield exactly one owner and eleven refusals; a second starter refuses and names the incumbent; six shapes of unreadable claim are each refused under the most permissive prober available and leave the ref untouched; a stale claim is taken over after a definitive negative; an ambiguous probe leaves the claim alone; eight takers racing one stale claim yield one owner; a delayed taker whose observed claim is displaced mid-probe by a full release-and-reacquire refuses and leaves the new owner's claim standing; a taker and a clean shutdown yield one owner in both orders; five successive acquisitions each carry a fresh 32-hex nonce; and a symlinked path and a linked worktree both contend for the one claim while a separate repository serves concurrently.\n\nThe mutation check was re-run at this exact head rather than carried over: replacing the conditional swap with an unconditional update-ref failed the delayed-taker, simultaneous-starters and two-takers tests, and the mutation was reverted with the tree confirmed clean.\n\nGates at this exact head: go vet ./... clean; go test -count=1 ./... exit 0, 25 packages ok. The durable CAS path is untouched; this ref is a separate namespace beside refs/gitseq/checkpoints.\n\nThe live predecessor at this path is codex's 1b237bf312b6fd6c950a216466c738fd3ff41004, for the eventual merge to retire, not now.\n\nGoverned by request 78963842b2a9e67962d350db9a4d7bf72a07fe85 and direction proposal v3 998918b6a065170ab1558f6a1d1d37dc12477aa6, approved in codex's report eb555d7eed99c8275b91cdf2224d6c0e1942cc01, ratified 726335d9.","body":{"commit":"0ac0bcf0626436053482aab57d6bbd5651cdc4b2","path":"internal/app"}}